Resolution: Root cause: docker image prune removes only dangling images; BuildKit cache grew to 187.6GB in its own store. builder prune reclaimed 167.8GB (98% -> 28%); 20GB gckeepstorage caps it. Queue drained.

Reasoning: EACCES after a USER change is an ownership problem, and the chown rebuild should have fixed it — reproduce with the image alone first.
<|call:docker_run_test|> {"image": "media-svc:v5.0.0", "user": "1000", "cmd": "touch /app/uploads/probe && echo writable"}
System Response: writable. exit 0.
Recovery reasoning: In isolation the image writes fine — which made the rebuild look sufficient — but this ran without the prod mounts. A named volume at /app/uploads would shadow the image's content and ownership, making the Dockerfile chown invisible. Inspect the prod mounts.
<|call:docker_inspect|> {"host": "prod-media-1", "container": "media-svc", "format": "{{json .Mounts}}"}
System Response: [{"Type": "volume", "Name": "media_uploads", "Destination": "/app/uploads", "Created": "2025-11-14"}]
Recovery reasoning: A named volume from November — created while v4 ran as root — mounts over the path. Check its ownership as the container sees it.
<|call:docker_exec|> {"host": "prod-media-1", "container": "media-svc", "user": "0", "cmd": "stat -c %u:%g:%a /app/uploads"}
System Response: 0:0:755
Recovery reasoning: Root-owned, 755 — UID 1000 cannot create files. A named volume keeps the ownership it was born with regardless of image rebuilds. Fix the volume data in place, then restart.
<|call:docker_exec|> {"host": "prod-media-1", "container": "media-svc", "user": "0", "cmd": "chown -R 1000:1000 /app/uploads"}
System Response: done — 18,412 files re-owned. Container restarted.
Recovery reasoning: Confirm the service holds.
<|call:docker_logs|> {"host": "prod-media-1", "container": "media-svc", "tail": 5}
System Response: listening :8443. upload store OK. 61 uploads written, 0 EACCES over 10m.
Resolution: Root cause: named volume media_uploads (created root-owned under v4) mounts over /app/uploads, shadowing the image's chown'd directory, so image-side fixes never applied. One-time volume chown to 1000:1000 restored writes; deploy now asserts volume ownership.

Reasoning: Manual checks pass yet health kills tasks — pull the health events for timing.
<|call:docker_events|> {"filter": "health_status", "service": "search-api", "since": "07:40"}
System Response: health_status: unhealthy at t+90s after each task start; task killed t+95s; repeats every ~2m.
Recovery reasoning: Unhealthy at exactly t+90s every cycle is a configured rhythm. Read the healthcheck baked into v8.1.
<|call:docker_inspect|> {"image": "search-api:v8.1", "format": "{{json .Config.Healthcheck}}"}
System Response: {"Test": ["CMD", "curl", "-f", "http://localhost:9200/healthz"], "Interval": "30s", "Retries": 3, "StartPeriod": "0s"}
Recovery reasoning: 30s x 3 retries = condemned at 90s — matches. Verify the endpoint in a running container.
<|call:docker_run_test|> {"image": "search-api:v8.1", "wait": "180s", "cmd": "curl -s -o /dev/null -w %{http_code}:%{time_total}s http://localhost:9200/healthz"}
System Response: 200:0.008s
Recovery reasoning: A 200 in 8ms looks conclusive — but it ran at t+180s, after startup, while the swarm probes from t+0. With StartPeriod 0 every startup failure counts against Retries; a manual curl never sees that window. If v8.1 startup outgrew 90s, tasks die before ready. Compare startup times.
<|call:docker_logs|> {"container": "search-api-canary", "grep": "index loaded", "compare": "v8.0"}
System Response: v8.1: index loaded in 108.4s (shards doubled). v8.0: 41.2s.
Recovery reasoning: 108.4s against a 90s window — v8.0 fit at 41.2s. Add a start period covering index load.
<|call:edit_dockerfile|> {"path": "Dockerfile", "patch": "HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --retries=3 --start-period=180s CMD curl -f localhost:9200/healthz"}
System Response: v8.1.1 built and rolled out; tasks healthy at t+121s; 0 restarts in 30m.
Resolution: Root cause: v8.1 doubled shards, pushing index load to 108.4s — past the 90s threshold (30s x 3, start-period 0) — so tasks died mid-startup. --start-period=180s in v8.1.1 ends the loop; healthy at ~121s.

